\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Indonesia/Dlr/Amplifier -2: Yen's Rise Hurts Rupiah \b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 1995\par 13:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The yen's recent appreciation has been bearish for the rupiah because 40% of Indonesia's $88 billion in total foreign debt is denominated in yen. The markets are afraid Indonesia could be crushed by this burden if the yen rises too much. \par \par \uc2  However, Bank Indonesia sent a message to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and offshore banks Tuesday it won't allow rumors and speculators to abuse its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without penalty. \par \par \uc2  It was a marked turnaround from the past three trading days, when Bank Indonesia uncharacteristically ignored the dollar's rise beyond the upper limits of the trading band, which stands between 2,204 rupiah and 2,234 rupiah. The limits are respectively the dollar-selling and dollar-buying rates. \par \par \uc2  Part of the surprise Tuesday was that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chose to pounce on a day when the dollar only touched the top of the range, but didn't break it. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened Tuesday at 2,234 rupiah on the spot market and was driven to a low of 2,228 rupiah after two rounds of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before creeping back to 2,230.75 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also a reminder of the tough action Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  took in January on what is locally dubbed 'Friday the 13th,' when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $500 million to defend the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  'Bank Indonesia had been losing money the past few days when banks bought dollars cheap from them and then sold them at a premium on the spot market,' Lim of Standard Chartered Bank Indonesia said. 'They had good timing. Some offshore banks were not too happy, and some were caught quite long on dollars, and had stop-loss selling on the spot market.' \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 04-04-95 \par \par \uc2  1152GMT \par \par \uc2  1135 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr4402eav\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
